WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.480
Hey there, welcome to Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 118. It's July 30th, 2026. Some awesome ai developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it. Here's something that could reshape how you spend your time in just a few years. Mark Zuckerberg expects billions of people to have their own personal ai agents within five years. These agents are ai systems built to handle everyday tasks for you.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:55.840
Such as managing schedules, answering questions, or guiding daily decisions. Meta is investing billions to build the systems that will run these agents at large scale. Zuckerberg is explaining this spending to investors during earnings calls, showing how the costs should lead to future returns. Think of an agent like a super smart digital assistant that learns your habits the way your phone keyboard learns the words you type most often.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:22.380
The key difference is that these agents could connect across many parts of your life instead of staying inside one single app. Meta also sees a bigger business chance that includes not only the agents themselves, but also the tools and computing power that let other companies build on the same foundation. This matters because it could change how students study, how families organize chores, or how people explore creative hobbies.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:41.820
Instead of searching the web yourself, you might ask your agent to pull together notes for a school project or suggest music based on your mood. The prediction arrives while Meta competes directly with open ai and anthropic on model development, which means the agents could improve quickly as the base models grow stronger.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:55.060
For teens and young people, it raises questions about privacy and control, such as who decides what the agent remembers about you. It also opens doors to new kinds of jobs helping design or guide these agents.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:03.860
The five-year timeline is ambitious, yet it gives a concrete window for thinking about what skills will be useful when personal agents become common.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:16.720
You can start exploring the idea today by trying free ai chat bots and noticing what they already do well or miss. Go to the chat GPT website or Claude site on your phone or laptop.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:27.020
Start a new chat and ask it to plan a perfect weekend based on three things you enjoy. See how it builds on your answers and where it still needs more guidance from you.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:36.600
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood. Today we are unpacking what open weights ai actually means.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:44.660
You know how when you share a photo with friends, some apps let anyone download and edit it while others keep it private to just your group.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:53.760
Open weights ai works in a similar way. The weights are the numbers inside an ai model that decide how it answers questions or creates images.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:01.260
When a company releases those numbers publicly, anyone can download the model and run it on their own computer or phone.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:08.400
That choice is now central to debates in Silicon Valley about how artificial intelligence software should be created.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:15.000
Students or hobbyists can experiment without paying for cloud access every time they want to test something.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:21.360
It also lets people check exactly how the ai behaves instead of trusting a company's version alone.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.660
Because the numbers are visible, researchers can test whether the model repeats certain patterns or makes particular mistakes.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.000
The trade-off is that open models sometimes need more technical setup than simple web chat bots.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:42.820
Still, they give more people a chance to understand and improve the technology instead of only big companies controlling it.

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:52.920
Once you see that the weights are just adjustable numbers, the whole idea stops feeling mysterious and starts feeling like sharing a recipe instead of keeping it locked in a vault.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:04:00.840
You can then decide for yourself whether the benefits of openness outweigh the extra effort required to run the model locally.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.740
That brings us to cool stuff and try this.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:11.320
First up is an experiment that turns classic poems into wild modern scenes using an image generator called flux 3.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:18.340
This tool can take tricky text and turn it into pictures that capture shifting moods and language surprisingly well.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:28.060
In one test, someone fed it lines from TS Eliot's poem The Wasteland and asked it to show a modern version of the Fisher King watching a city fall apart.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:37.380
The prompt included lines about sitting upon the shore fishing with an arid plane behind, plus closing fragments about London bridge and swallows.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.980
The results mix the old words with new visuals in striking ways.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:51.400
This is exciting because it shows how AI can help with creative projects like book covers, storyboards or fan art without needing years of drawing practice.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.080
Anyone who likes writing stories, making tiktoks or designing posters should try it.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.100
Search online for a free flux 3 image generator demo.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:09.360
Go to that demo, paste the last few lines of a poem or song you like and add the words in a modern city at night at the end.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.380
Watch how the AI mixes the old words with new visuals and notice which phrases it turns into striking images.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.600
Next is a tool called Pangram that checks whether text was written by AI.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:28.600
It claims to detect almost all AI generated text while making very few mistakes on real human writing.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:34.400
The model is also designed to resist tools that try to disguise AI writing as human.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.640
This matters for students who want to understand how much of what they read online might be machine made.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:47.760
You can test short paragraphs you write yourself versus text from a chatbot to see the difference in detection scores.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:54.340
Search online for the Pangram detector and paste a paragraph from an AI chat into the tool.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.180
Compare the result with a paragraph you wrote yourself to see how the scores differ.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:05.880
Time for quick bits. Consumer reports looked at whether chat bots are reliable for medical questions.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:14.860
The answer is still complicated because AI can give general information but often misses important personal details that a doctor would catch.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:27.120
The organization tested several popular chat bots and found that while they can list common symptoms they rarely ask follow-up questions that would narrow down causes the way a real medical professional would.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:34.560
A startup called Encore is building AI agents that study real customer conversations to copy what works.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:41.780
The goal is to help businesses train better helpers but it also shows how agents can improve by watching humans.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:53.340
The company raised $30 million to analyze calls, messages and customer data so the agents can identify effective sales techniques and turn them into reusable playbooks.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:59.240
That's it for today. Remember every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.360
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.860
Stay curious, keep experimenting and we'll see you tomorrow.

